  1	// PlanB64: offline IPv6 FIB compiler and longest-prefix lookup tool.
  2	//
  3	// Longest-prefix matching over prefixes of length at most 64 is reduced to
  4	// predecessor search over disjoint intervals of the 64-bit upper-address key
  5	// space.  `build` compiles a FIB into the minimal change-point representation
  6	// of the function  key -> (next hop, prefix length);  `lookup` answers trace
  7	// queries with a binary search over the compiled rows.
  8	//
  9	// Compilation idea.  A prefix of length L covers the aligned dyadic key range
 10	// [start, start + 2^(64-L)).  Dyadic ranges are laminar: two of them either
 11	// nest or are disjoint.  Hence at any candidate change point the covering
 12	// prefixes form a chain ordered by length, so a single stack sweep over the
 13	// sorted candidate boundaries yields the winning route of every elementary
 14	// interval in O(n log n).  Candidate boundaries are exactly 0 plus every
 15	// prefix start plus every prefix end below 2^64, which bounds the row count by
 16	// 2*records + 1.
 17	
